; a link is the rigid body between two joints in a kinematic chain
; it carries no DOF itself — all motion happens at the joints
; 
; 3D perspective:
; the link IS a bone in the scene-graph — rigid segment with fixed rest-length
; its local coordinate frame is defined by its proximal joint
; its world-space pose is fully determined by all ancestor joint angles
; (this is the chala-apeksha chain: each ancestor's frame moves it)
; 
; physics perspective:
; a rigid body with mass, length, and inertia tensor
; for a uniform rod: I = (1/3)·m·L² about the proximal end
; the mass distribution determines how much torque is needed to accelerate it
; its velocity in world-space depends on ALL ancestor joint velocities
; (chala-apeksha: the transport theorem applies at every level)
; 
; in the scene: links are ordered by krama sequence — link[0] is base, link[n] is tip
; the end-effector bindu is the distal end of link[n] in world-space
; physical properties
; frame structure: link lives in its proximal joint's chala-apeksha
; its world pose is phala of all ancestor joint transforms
; ordering in the chain

(kosha link
  (sthita domain-robotics chala-apeksha parampara krama)
  (yukta kinematic-chain mass link-length moment-of-inertia inertia-tensor krama-janya)
  (abheda bone rekha)
  (phala world-space))
